      "skill": "command-of-evidence-textual",
      "passage": "On clear, calm spring nights, the coldest air in an orchard settles near the ground while air a hundred feet up can be several degrees warmer — a layering called an inversion. Growers run tall fans on such nights to protect blossoms from frost, and a team of researchers has proposed an explanation of how the fans work: rather than warming anything themselves, the fans pull the warmer air of the inversion layer down and mix it through the trees. If the researchers are right, the fans' protection should depend on that warm layer being there to mix.",
