      "correctAnswer": "D",
      "explanation": "**Choice D is correct.** The hypothesis says the fans work by mixing down the inversion's warm air, so their effect should appear when the warm layer exists and vanish when it does not — exactly the pattern choice D reports.\n\n**The Full Solution:**\n- Pin down the claim precisely: the fans do not warm anything; they redistribute warmth that the inversion layer supplies.\n- The passage even states the test: protection \"should depend on that warm layer being there to mix.\"\n- Choice D runs that test in both directions. Warm layer present: fans raise blossom-height temperatures. Warm layer absent: the same fans change nothing.\n- An effect that switches on and off with the warm layer ties the fans' benefit to the layer itself, which is the researchers' whole mechanism.\n- How to spot it: when a passage says a result \"should depend on\" some condition, the strongest support shows the result appearing with the condition and disappearing without it.\n\n**Why the other choices are wrong:**\n- A: Hillside orchards relate to where cold air settles, but the finding never mentions fans, so it cannot support a claim about how fans work.\n- B: The hardiness of buds versus blossoms says nothing about what the fans are doing to the air.\n- C: Reports that orchards fare \"about the same either way\" on windy nights are loose observations without temperature measurements — consistent with the hypothesis, but far less direct than choice D's measured on-off pattern."
