      "correctAnswer": "B",
      "explanation": "**Choice B is correct.** The sentence is inverted — the subject comes after the verb — and that subject is the plural \"several star clusters,\" so the verb must be \"are.\"\n\n**The Full Solution:**\n- Flip the sentence back into normal order to expose the subject: \"Several star clusters ... are among the rewards awaiting anyone who sweeps the winter sky.\"\n- Once flipped, the subject is plainly the plural \"clusters\" — not the singular-sounding nouns near the blank like \"sky\" or \"binoculars,\" which sit inside modifying phrases.\n- The sentence describes a standing fact about the winter sky, so the plural present \"are\" completes it.\n- How to spot it: when a sentence opens with \"Among the...\" or \"Beneath the...\", the subject follows the verb — find it on the far side of the blank before choosing.\n\n**Why the other choices are wrong:**\n- A: \"Is\" is singular; it wrongly agrees with a nearby noun instead of the true subject, \"clusters.\"\n- C: \"Has been\" is also singular, and its perfect tense adds a time shift the sentence gives no reason for.\n- D: \"Being\" is not a working verb, so the sentence would be left with no main verb at all."
