      "correctAnswer": "A",
      "explanation": "**Choice A is correct.** The subject is the plural \"props,\" so the verb must be the plural \"keep.\"\n\n**The Full Solution:**\n- Find the true subject by stripping the modifiers: \"The wooden props ... keep the weight ... from splitting the trunk.\"\n- Everything between subject and blank — \"wedged beneath an old apple tree's heaviest branches, adjusted a little each season as the limbs spread\" — only describes the props; none of those nouns controls the verb.\n- The sentence also needs a main verb in the present, matching the ongoing arrangement it describes, and plural \"keep\" agrees with \"props.\"\n- How to spot it: cross out every phrase set off by commas between the subject and the blank, then match the verb to the noun that remains.\n\n**Why the other choices are wrong:**\n- B: \"Keeps\" is singular; it wrongly agrees with a nearby singular noun like \"tree\" or \"season\" instead of the subject, \"props.\"\n- C: \"Keeping\" is an -ing form that cannot serve as the sentence's main verb, leaving the sentence without one.\n- D: \"To keep\" is an infinitive and likewise leaves the sentence with no main verb."
