      "correctAnswer": "B",
      "explanation": "**Choice B is correct.** Two complete sentences meet at the blank, and a period is a correct way to separate them.\n\n**The Full Solution:**\n- Test each side of the blank on its own. Before: \"the pause between scenes is rehearsed as carefully as the scenes themselves\" — a complete sentence. After: \"Crew members practice each move with the lights up...\" — also complete.\n- Two independent clauses need a real boundary: a period, a semicolon, or a comma paired with a joining word such as \"and.\"\n- Choice B supplies the period and capitalizes the new sentence cleanly.\n- How to spot it: read each side of the blank aloud as its own sentence; if both stand alone, a comma by itself can never join them.\n\n**Why the other choices are wrong:**\n- A: A comma alone between two complete sentences is a comma splice.\n- C: With no punctuation at all, the two sentences run together.\n- D: \"And\" could join the clauses only with a comma BEFORE it; putting the comma after \"and\" cuts the joining word off from the clause it introduces."
