      "explanation": "**Choice D is correct.** The words before the blank form a complete sentence, and what follows is the list of the three things it promises — the job a colon is built for.\n\n**The Full Solution:**\n- Check the left side first: \"Naturalists who lead tide-pool walks tell first-time visitors to bring three things\" can stand alone as a sentence.\n- What follows spells out those three things in order: the shoes, the notebook, the tide table.\n- A colon after a complete sentence introduces exactly this kind of spelled-out list.\n- How to spot it: if you could stop the sentence at the blank and what comes next answers \"namely, which ones?\", the mark you want is a colon.\n\n**Why the other choices are wrong:**\n- A: A semicolon joins two complete sentences, and the list of items is not a sentence.\n- B: With no punctuation, the list crashes into the sentence with nothing to introduce it.\n- C: A comma is too weak to introduce a list after a complete sentence — especially a list whose own items are separated by commas."
