      "explanation": "**Choice C is correct.** The sentence pins its action to a specific past time — 1911 — so the verb must be the simple past, \"produced.\"\n\n**The Full Solution:**\n- Find the sentence's time frame first. The opening clause supplies it: \"When the editor ... installed ... in 1911.\"\n- An action tied to a named past date takes the simple past; the main clause reports what she did then.\n- \"Produced\" matches \"installed,\" keeping both verbs in the same past moment.\n- How to spot it: a specific date in the sentence is a signal for the simple past — not a present form, and not a \"has\"-form, which connects an action to the present.\n\n**Why the other choices are wrong:**\n- A: Present-tense \"produces\" clashes with the 1911 time frame the sentence establishes.\n- B: \"Is producing\" is likewise present tense, describing an action happening now.\n- D: \"Has produced\" links an action to the present moment, which contradicts an event fixed at a specific past date."
